## 5. Git & Contribution Workflow

Since you forked from your friend's repository, your local Git state is set up as follows:
- **`origin`**: Your repository clone (`https://github.com/Vaibhavlanjewar/MailPilot.git`). This is where you have push access.
- **`upstream`**: Your friend's original repository (`https://github.com/pnkjshahare/MailPilot.git`). This is where the source code originates from.

To manage contributions neatly without git conflicts, follow this workflow:

### 1. Sync Your Fork with Upstream Changes
Before starting any new feature or fix, pull the latest changes from the original (upstream) repo:
```bash
# 1. Fetch all upstream branches
git fetch upstream

# 2. Make sure you are on the main branch
git checkout main

# 3. Merge the changes from upstream/main into your local main branch
git merge upstream/main

# 4. Push the updated main to your personal GitHub origin
git push origin main
```

### 2. Create a Feature/Topic Branch
Never work directly on the `main` branch. Create a descriptive branch for your changes:
```bash
git checkout -b feature/your-feature-name
# or
git checkout -b fix/bug-description
```

### 3. Develop & Stage Changes
Make your code changes, and test locally (see Local Setup below). Once verified:
```bash
# Check modified files
git status

# Stage the file changes
git add .

# Commit with a descriptive message
git commit -m "feat: implement AI template generation with smart fallback"
```

### 4. Push to Your Fork
Push your branch to your own GitHub repository (`origin`):
```bash
git push -u origin feature/your-feature-name
```

### 5. Submit a Pull Request (PR)
1. Go to your repository on GitHub: `https://github.com/Vaibhavlanjewar/MailPilot`.
2. A banner will pop up suggesting you create a **Pull Request**.
3. Set the base repository to `pnkjshahare/MailPilot` (branch: `main`) and the head repository to your fork `Vaibhavlanjewar/MailPilot` (branch: `feature/your-feature-name`).
4. Describe your changes clearly and submit the PR for your friend to review.

